            <ArticleTitle>Self-warming lidocaine/tetracaine patch effectively and safely induces local anesthesia during minor dermatologic procedures.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Dermatologic procedures often cause some degree of pain. A self-warming patch containing lidocaine and tetracaine (L/T) was developed to provide topical local anesthesia prior to painful procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the safety and efficacy of a self-warming L/T patch to provide anesthesia in adult patients undergoing minor dermatologic procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">An active or placebo study drug was placed on adults 30 minutes prior to minor dermatologic surgical procedures in a prospectively randomized, double-blinded manner. Subcutaneous lidocaine injection was available during the procedure as a rescue medication if requested by the subject. Immediately following the procedure, the subjects, the investigator, and an independent observer rated pain intensity and adverse events were recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patient-reported pain intensity was significantly lower in the L/T patch group (p&lt;.001). Investigators and an independent observer rated the pain in the L/T patch group to be less than in the placebo patch group (p = .004 and p&lt;.001, respectively). Forty-nine percent of patients in the placebo group required rescue subcutaneous lidocaine compared with 22% in the L/T patch study group (p = .008). One patient in the L/T patch group reported a transient moderate burning sensation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The self-warming L/T patch was effective in providing clinically useful local anesthesia for minor dermatologic procedures in adult patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
